    "Measured on 2026-09-02. `src/` holds 58 TypeScript files and 1,356,243 bytes. Two files carry 1,120,106 of those bytes, 82.6 per cent: `src/eso-base-game-globals.generated.ts` at 788,312 and `src/eso-colon-methods.generated.ts` at 331,794, whose header states 12,854 distinct colon-method names. Both are written by `tools/commands/eso/generate-base-game-globals.ts` and `tools/commands/eso/generate-colon-methods.ts`. The third generated file, `src/tstl-colon-dot-self-shift.baseline.generated.ts`, is 3,558 bytes and fits.\n\nThe ceiling alone would be answerable. `akasha/temper/temper-eso-types` already carries 524,347 bytes as 71 page folders with no file over the ceiling, `eso-enums-01` through `eso-enums-19` and `eso-functions-01` onward, each packed to just under the line. About 76 more chunks would hold these two.\n\nWhat cannot be answered mechanically is the disagreement. `temper-eso-types` declares the 6,290 ESO names temper's own addons call, 188 of them `SI_*` string ids, and `eso-reserved-colon-methods` holds 18 colon methods. The generated pair is a census of what the base game has: 20,963 names, 13,108 `SI_*` ids, 12,854 colon methods. `check-tstl-this-void-colon-method` asks whether a receiver is a base-game global, and against the curated set it answers no for 14,673 names that are. Two authorities in one tree would contradict each other by construction.\n\nFourteen of the 58 files landed at a1b04a5aaa and 28dff92e5c, none reading the census. The families that read it are still out.\n\nThe package ships zero tests, where akasha's checks run tests two to three times the size of the code.\n\nWhat could land without touching the census: `src/lib/*`, `population-bound`, `ts-node-shapes`, `addon-removed-refs`, `addon-dependency-cycle`, `tstl-range-double-shift`, `tstl-anytable-length` and `addon-roster-guard`, about a dozen modules.",
